WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.493
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:39.240
NAUTA: I am Dr. Walle Nauta.

00:01:39.240 --> 00:01:44.670
We have now arrived at the third
tape in our brain dissection

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:48.300
course, in which
we will be dealing

00:01:48.300 --> 00:01:51.196
with the cranial nerves.

00:01:51.196 --> 00:01:54.542
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.380
The same ventral
aspect of the brain

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:16.710
that gave us such a good view
of the great arterial vessels

00:02:16.710 --> 00:02:19.340
supplying the brain--

00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:21.780
that same ventral surface--

00:02:21.780 --> 00:02:35.520
will also show almost the entire
contingent of cranial nerves

00:02:35.520 --> 00:02:40.060
leaving or entering the brain.

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:46.020
Let us try to identify some
of these cranial nerves.

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:51.780
Here again, it is
a bit of a problem

00:02:51.780 --> 00:02:57.670
to do it before the
arachnoid has been removed.

00:02:57.670 --> 00:03:00.930
But if we postpone it until
after the arachnoid has

00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:06.720
been removed, we risk losing
the more fragile rootlets

00:03:06.720 --> 00:03:08.310
of some cranial nerves.

00:03:08.310 --> 00:03:12.570
That is why a preliminary
survey of the cranial nerves

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:16.080
is most usefully
done at this stage.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:20.500
Let us have a look
at the cranial nerves

00:03:20.500 --> 00:03:25.260
that we can see upon
inspecting the ventral aspect

00:03:25.260 --> 00:03:27.550
of the brain.

00:03:27.550 --> 00:03:36.630
It will be necessary to look for
these cranial nerves in three

00:03:36.630 --> 00:03:38.310
different brains.

00:03:38.310 --> 00:03:41.920
It is quite rare
to find all of them

00:03:41.920 --> 00:03:45.660
intact and optimally
demonstrable

00:03:45.660 --> 00:03:46.950
in a single brain--

00:03:46.950 --> 00:03:50.390
so you would understand
if, from time to time,

00:03:50.390 --> 00:03:52.820
the specimen is
going to be changed.

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:04.440
Starting at the caudal
end of the brain,

00:04:04.440 --> 00:04:10.410
at the spinomedullary
junction, we

00:04:10.410 --> 00:04:15.960
note that many cranial
nerves make their exit

00:04:15.960 --> 00:04:22.670
or enter in the most posterior
parts of the brainstem

00:04:22.670 --> 00:04:26.780
and the rhombencephalon.

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:31.140
The most important
orienting line

00:04:31.140 --> 00:04:37.710
is this border line here
between medulla oblongata

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:41.370
and the pontine protuberance.

00:04:41.370 --> 00:04:46.840
Then we start at that line,
the so-called pontomedullary

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:48.450
sulcus--

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:53.180
we can count three
great cranial nerves.

00:04:53.180 --> 00:05:00.080
The one closest to
the midline is here,

00:05:00.080 --> 00:05:03.670
this is the abducens nerve.

00:05:03.670 --> 00:05:06.430
Somewhat farther out
laterally, emerging

00:05:06.430 --> 00:05:09.730
through the same deep
groove between the pons

00:05:09.730 --> 00:05:15.506
and the medulla oblongata, is
number VII, the facial nerve.

00:05:15.506 --> 00:05:19.030
And finally, if I were
to start laterally,

00:05:19.030 --> 00:05:24.730
here is a double
nerve that is actually

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:31.000
composed of the cochlear nerve
with the vestibular nerve.

00:05:31.000 --> 00:05:35.920
I have it here, and I hope
it is somewhat visible.

00:05:35.920 --> 00:05:44.070
It is this double nerve here,
number VIII, the nervus octavus

00:05:44.070 --> 00:05:45.560
or statoacusticus.

00:05:49.820 --> 00:05:55.510
VI, VII, and VIII,
abducens, facial,

00:05:55.510 --> 00:06:00.370
and statoacusticus here.

00:06:00.370 --> 00:06:05.320
If you now go in a straight
line down from that grove here,

00:06:05.320 --> 00:06:06.639
we encounter--

00:06:06.639 --> 00:06:07.555
close to the midline--

00:06:10.220 --> 00:06:12.760
a cranial nerve that
is characterized

00:06:12.760 --> 00:06:19.300
by appearing on the surface
in quite a large series

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.690
of little fascicles
of rootlets here.

00:06:22.690 --> 00:06:29.410
This is cranial nerve number
XII, the hypoglossal nerve.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:33.130
The hypoglossal nerve, as you
can rather clearly see in this

00:06:33.130 --> 00:06:40.850
specimen, emerges from just
underneath this olive-shaped--

00:06:40.850 --> 00:06:50.160
this overt bulge here, the
so-called inferior olive.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:58.600
On its medial side here
runs the pyramidal tract.

00:06:58.600 --> 00:07:04.660
It is through that groove
between the olivary bulge

00:07:04.660 --> 00:07:09.790
and the pyramid, that the
hypoglossal nerve makes

00:07:09.790 --> 00:07:12.400
its exit from the brainstem.

00:07:12.400 --> 00:07:16.840
It is a pure motor nerve
that innervates the tongue.

00:07:19.570 --> 00:07:23.480
A little bit farther
laterally here,

00:07:23.480 --> 00:07:26.960
through the side of
the medulla oblongata

00:07:26.960 --> 00:07:31.730
and taking their exit
through a line quite a bit

00:07:31.730 --> 00:07:35.100
dorsal to the olivary
prominence here,

00:07:35.100 --> 00:07:41.690
are two further nerves: IX,
the glossopharyngeus here,

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:45.230
and its immediate
caudal neighbor--

00:07:45.230 --> 00:07:47.520
the somewhat larger nerve--

00:07:47.520 --> 00:07:54.020
that we call nervus
vagus, IX and X here.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:58.510
Let me count the nerves once
more here, name them by number.

00:07:58.510 --> 00:08:03.830
Here in the pontomedullary
grove from medial to lateral:

00:08:03.830 --> 00:08:08.330
the abduncens nerve
here, the facial nerve--

00:08:08.330 --> 00:08:12.610
number VII-- and
this double nerve,

00:08:12.610 --> 00:08:16.400
the statoacoustic nerve--
composed of the cochlear

00:08:16.400 --> 00:08:18.284
and the vestibular nerve--

00:08:18.284 --> 00:08:19.610
here.

00:08:19.610 --> 00:08:26.390
Just behind that VIII nerve
emerges cranial nerve number

00:08:26.390 --> 00:08:30.050
VIIII, the glossopharyngeus.

00:08:30.050 --> 00:08:35.000
And immediately in line
with it and like it,

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:37.970
emerging through the lateral
surface of the medulla

00:08:37.970 --> 00:08:45.290
oblongata, is the vagus,
number X. Here is number XII--

00:08:45.290 --> 00:08:49.160
and you may now ask,
where is number XI?

00:08:49.160 --> 00:08:52.535
Where is the 11th cranial nerve?

00:08:52.535 --> 00:08:59.540
The 11th cranial nerve
is a most devious one.

00:08:59.540 --> 00:09:04.840
It is actually, to the
largest extent at least,

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:07.931
a spinal cord nerve.

00:09:07.931 --> 00:09:13.490
However, in its course, it
enters the cranial cavity

00:09:13.490 --> 00:09:16.070
through the foramen
magnum, and it

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:21.770
emerges in the company of the
glossopharyngeus and the vagus

00:09:21.770 --> 00:09:26.960
through a common opening in
the base of the cranial cavity,

00:09:26.960 --> 00:09:30.600
the so-called jugular foramen.

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:34.940
IX, the glossopharyngeus,
X, the vagus, and XI,

00:09:34.940 --> 00:09:39.530
the accessory nerve
here, emerge together

00:09:39.530 --> 00:09:43.840
from the cranial cavity
through the jugular foramen.

00:09:43.840 --> 00:09:51.020
You see here, only a short
stretch of the accessory nerve.

00:09:51.020 --> 00:09:54.890
I must pull the vertical
artery a little bit

00:09:54.890 --> 00:09:57.770
toward the medial
side to show it.

00:09:57.770 --> 00:10:03.280
This nerve, here running
alongside the spinal cord

00:10:03.280 --> 00:10:07.550
really, is not in the typical
position of a ventral root

00:10:07.550 --> 00:10:10.030
of the spinal cord--

00:10:10.030 --> 00:10:13.170
here is a ventral root
of the spinal cord--

00:10:13.170 --> 00:10:15.770
nor does it correspond
in its line of exit

00:10:15.770 --> 00:10:18.890
to a dorsal root
of the spinal cord.

00:10:18.890 --> 00:10:23.870
It follows an intermediate
position here,

00:10:23.870 --> 00:10:27.970
emerging from the
side of the upper five

00:10:27.970 --> 00:10:31.670
to six spinal cord segments.

00:10:31.670 --> 00:10:37.100
These rootlets of
the accessory nerve

00:10:37.100 --> 00:10:43.340
join each other into this
longitudinal strand here,

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:50.030
that then quite anomalously--

00:10:50.030 --> 00:10:54.610
quite atypical for a
spinal cord nerve here--

00:10:54.610 --> 00:10:59.898
enters the cranial cavity
through the foramen magnum.

00:10:59.898 --> 00:11:04.870
Now I'll just quickly review
these cranial nerves again.

00:11:04.870 --> 00:11:13.110
Through the pontomedullary
sulcus emerge cranial nerve

00:11:13.110 --> 00:11:15.300
VI-- the abducens--

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.140
VII-- the facial--

00:11:19.650 --> 00:11:25.910
VIII-- the statoacoustic
nerve here.

00:11:25.910 --> 00:11:30.330
Going straight back from
the nerve number VIII--

00:11:30.330 --> 00:11:32.000
the statoacoustic--

00:11:32.000 --> 00:11:36.080
emerge the cranial nerves,
IX, the glossopharyngeus here,

00:11:36.080 --> 00:11:39.740
and immediately
associated with it

00:11:39.740 --> 00:11:44.550
on the caudal side, the somewhat
larger cranial nerve number X,

00:11:44.550 --> 00:11:46.190
the vagus.

00:11:46.190 --> 00:11:53.060
Cranial nerve number XI
originates from the spinal cord

00:11:53.060 --> 00:11:57.110
and joins IX and X
from the caudal side

00:11:57.110 --> 00:12:01.360
to leave the cranium
through the jugular foramen.

00:12:01.360 --> 00:12:05.570
Here, as the only
nerve to emerge

00:12:05.570 --> 00:12:11.120
from below the olive,
the hypoglossal nerve

00:12:11.120 --> 00:12:14.450
with its many
rootlets here, that

00:12:14.450 --> 00:12:18.650
come together to form the
quite substantial motor

00:12:18.650 --> 00:12:22.100
nerve innervating the
tongue musculature.

00:12:22.100 --> 00:12:30.290
This small territory here of
the upper medulla oblongata

00:12:30.290 --> 00:12:33.590
and to lower border
of the pons, really

00:12:33.590 --> 00:12:37.079
is the site of exit
of no less than 1,

00:12:37.079 --> 00:12:42.575
2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7 cranial nerves.

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:49.970
By comparison, the other
parts of the brain surface

00:12:49.970 --> 00:12:54.460
are really much more sparsely
supplied by cranial nerves.

00:12:54.460 --> 00:13:00.830
When I say supplied, I should
have said sparsely used as exit

00:13:00.830 --> 00:13:04.820
or entrance by cranial nerves.

00:13:04.820 --> 00:13:07.280
However, the largest
cranial nerve--

00:13:07.280 --> 00:13:09.890
number V, the trigeminus--

00:13:09.890 --> 00:13:14.540
is seen here in a slightly
more forward position entering

00:13:14.540 --> 00:13:16.810
the brain through
the brachium pontis,

00:13:16.810 --> 00:13:25.886
or the middle cerrebellar
peduncle here.

00:13:25.886 --> 00:13:30.150
The trigeminus is very
largely a sensory nerve,

00:13:30.150 --> 00:13:32.840
but it has a small
motor component

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:37.760
that sometimes forms a
separate little rootlet here

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:40.700
on the ventral and medial side.

00:13:40.700 --> 00:13:43.640
In this particular
case, I can not really

00:13:43.640 --> 00:13:48.560
say that I could identify
it with any certainty.

00:13:48.560 --> 00:13:52.070
It could be this one,
but I am not sure

00:13:52.070 --> 00:13:54.290
at all that that is true.

00:13:54.290 --> 00:13:57.980
The fifth cranial nerve,
a most remarkable nerve

00:13:57.980 --> 00:14:02.320
that breaks really all
the rules of nerve exit.

00:14:02.320 --> 00:14:08.420
Here is the cranial nerve
number IV, the trochlear nerve.

00:14:08.420 --> 00:14:12.620
The trochlear nerve originates
from the caudal part

00:14:12.620 --> 00:14:14.290
of the midbrain.

00:14:14.290 --> 00:14:20.150
It emerges immediately behind
the inferior colliculus.

00:14:20.150 --> 00:14:24.020
Through a very anomalous site--

00:14:24.020 --> 00:14:27.270
namely the dorsal surface--

00:14:27.270 --> 00:14:33.170
there are no other
motor nerves known

00:14:33.170 --> 00:14:37.390
to leave either the
brainstem or the spinal cord

00:14:37.390 --> 00:14:39.590
through the dorsal surface.

00:14:39.590 --> 00:14:43.190
It is really quite unique
to the trochlear nerve.

00:14:43.190 --> 00:14:51.320
And then to add an other
peculiarity, at its exit,

00:14:51.320 --> 00:14:55.550
it immediately crosses
over to the opposite side,

00:14:55.550 --> 00:14:59.040
so that the trochlear
nerve of the left side

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:04.481
innervates the superior oblique
muscle of the right eye.

00:15:04.481 --> 00:15:07.475
Let us see if we can
find the trochlear nerve.

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:14.670
It is deeply hidden
here in the depths

00:15:14.670 --> 00:15:17.440
of the transverse fissure.

00:15:17.440 --> 00:15:21.510
I can show it already,
where it emerges

00:15:21.510 --> 00:15:24.270
from around the cerebellum.

00:15:24.270 --> 00:15:26.180
It is here.

00:15:26.180 --> 00:15:29.910
I might even turn this back
to its original position,

00:15:29.910 --> 00:15:33.100
and here is the trochlear nerve.

00:15:33.100 --> 00:15:36.960
But its position at the
moment is quite deceptive.

00:15:36.960 --> 00:15:39.270
It does not emerge through
the ventral surface

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:42.680
of the brainstem, it comes
from the dorsal surface.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:46.560
And let us see if we
can follow it there

00:15:46.560 --> 00:15:48.915
and identify its
point of origin.

00:15:52.440 --> 00:15:57.270
It's a bit risky to pull off
the arachnoid under which it is

00:15:57.270 --> 00:16:01.770
covered here, but there it is.

00:16:01.770 --> 00:16:07.830
And here we see this
remarkable cranial nerve

00:16:07.830 --> 00:16:12.390
as it comes from the dorsal
surface of the midbrain here,

00:16:12.390 --> 00:16:15.890
runs circumferentially
over the lateral surface

00:16:15.890 --> 00:16:17.240
of the midbrain.

00:16:17.240 --> 00:16:23.120
And here appears, on the ventral
side, the trochlear nerve,

00:16:23.120 --> 00:16:25.640
number IV.

00:16:25.640 --> 00:16:32.270
The trochlear nerve innervates
one of the external eye

00:16:32.270 --> 00:16:33.890
muscles.

00:16:33.890 --> 00:16:37.580
Another one, the
lateral rectus muscle,

00:16:37.580 --> 00:16:41.810
is innervated by
the abducens nerve.

00:16:41.810 --> 00:16:49.710
The trochlear nerve innervates
a single external eye muscle,

00:16:49.710 --> 00:16:55.820
and that is the
superior oblique muscle.

00:16:55.820 --> 00:17:01.430
Then the remainder of the
external eye muscles--

00:17:01.430 --> 00:17:07.120
the rectus superior, the rectus
medialis, the rectus inferior,

00:17:07.120 --> 00:17:13.550
the obliquus inferior, and the
levator palpebrae superioris--

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:19.940
are all innervated by a single
cranial nerve, the oculomotor.

00:17:19.940 --> 00:17:23.000
The oculomotor nerve,
unfortunately in this specimen,

00:17:23.000 --> 00:17:24.300
is lacking.

00:17:24.300 --> 00:17:26.200
It undoubtedly must
have [INAUDIBLE]

00:17:26.200 --> 00:17:31.820
when the brain was lifted up
from inside the cranial cavity.

00:17:31.820 --> 00:17:36.420
And we will have to identify
it in another specimen.

00:17:36.420 --> 00:17:39.430
If I may ask for
another specimen please.

00:17:43.230 --> 00:17:44.180
Thank you.

00:17:47.520 --> 00:17:49.980
It is here.

00:17:49.980 --> 00:17:57.870
The oculomotor nerve is
really, as you can see here,

00:17:57.870 --> 00:18:01.170
an unexpectedly large nerve.

00:18:01.170 --> 00:18:06.810
For so little volume
of striated musculature

00:18:06.810 --> 00:18:12.620
as it has to innervate, its
size is truly remarkable.

00:18:12.620 --> 00:18:17.920
It all indicates that
the innervation ratio--

00:18:17.920 --> 00:18:24.450
nerve fiber versus number
of striated muscle fibers--

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:26.780
is really quite high here.

00:18:30.190 --> 00:18:33.970
Here, once more, is the
trochlear nerve emerging

00:18:33.970 --> 00:18:39.570
from around the midbrain here.

00:18:39.570 --> 00:18:41.950
Here is the fifth cranial nerve.

00:18:41.950 --> 00:18:44.980
Here is the oculomotor
nerve emerging

00:18:44.980 --> 00:18:50.830
just rostral to the
pons from the depths

00:18:50.830 --> 00:18:53.950
of the interpeduncular
fossa here,

00:18:53.950 --> 00:18:58.510
and making its exit
characteristically

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:01.660
between the superior
cerebellar artery

00:19:01.660 --> 00:19:06.520
immediately behind it and
the posterior cerebral artery

00:19:06.520 --> 00:19:09.350
immediately in front of it.

00:19:09.350 --> 00:19:13.960
This is really the typical
position of the oculomotor

00:19:13.960 --> 00:19:17.290
nerve at its exit,
almost pinched

00:19:17.290 --> 00:19:20.480
between the two end branches
of the basilar artery--

00:19:24.930 --> 00:19:28.720
the superior cerebellar
artery and the posterior

00:19:28.720 --> 00:19:30.830
cerebral artery here.

00:19:33.690 --> 00:19:37.245
What now remains in the
way of cranial nerves

00:19:37.245 --> 00:19:41.940
are two cranial nerves that
were counted as number I and II,

00:19:41.940 --> 00:19:45.600
although later it became
obvious that they are not really

00:19:45.600 --> 00:19:51.780
nerves in the strict
sense of the word.

00:19:51.780 --> 00:19:56.640
The two nerves in question
are cranial nerves I and II.

00:19:56.640 --> 00:20:01.350
We find here the structure
that was described

00:20:01.350 --> 00:20:05.700
in original
anatomical dissection,

00:20:05.700 --> 00:20:08.400
as the first cranial nerve.

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:10.920
This is the olfactory bulb.

00:20:10.920 --> 00:20:13.590
This is the olfactory tract.

00:20:13.590 --> 00:20:16.980
And here is its attachment
to the ventral surface

00:20:16.980 --> 00:20:21.900
of the brain, a region known
as the olfactory trigone here.

00:20:21.900 --> 00:20:30.060
You can see the triangular
shape of the area there.

00:20:30.060 --> 00:20:33.480
The olfactory tract
inserts itself

00:20:33.480 --> 00:20:35.550
to the base of the brain.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:41.930
This then used to be called
cranial nerve number I. We now

00:20:41.930 --> 00:20:45.210
know that it is not
a nerve in the sense

00:20:45.210 --> 00:20:51.150
that it develops as part of
the central nervous system,

00:20:51.150 --> 00:20:54.660
and not in the form
of nerve fibers

00:20:54.660 --> 00:20:58.820
growing out or growing toward
the central nervous system.

00:21:04.640 --> 00:21:10.030
Cranial nerve number
II is the optic nerve

00:21:10.030 --> 00:21:17.890
that we see here, going
through its hemidecussation--

00:21:17.890 --> 00:21:22.140
immediately in front of
the pituitary stalk here

00:21:22.140 --> 00:21:24.310
there's a hemidecussation
between the right

00:21:24.310 --> 00:21:28.570
and the left optic nerve--

00:21:28.570 --> 00:21:33.070
to continue into the
optic tract here.

00:21:33.070 --> 00:21:41.530
This too was counted as a nerve,
although since that early time,

00:21:41.530 --> 00:21:43.840
it has come to be
recognized that this is not

00:21:43.840 --> 00:21:47.810
really a cranial nerve, but part
of the central nervous system.

00:21:47.810 --> 00:21:52.600
The retina and the optic
nerve are really outpouchings.

00:21:52.600 --> 00:21:57.990
They are everted portions of the
central nervous system itself--

00:21:57.990 --> 00:22:00.940
forwarded outposts
of the brain itself,

00:22:00.940 --> 00:22:03.325
rather than being a true nerve.

00:22:06.010 --> 00:22:09.412
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:22:19.640 --> 00:22:23.350
It may be useful
at this juncture--

00:22:23.350 --> 00:22:28.280
now that we have seen the
external appearance of some

00:22:28.280 --> 00:22:33.410
of the cranial nerves as they
make their exit from the brain

00:22:33.410 --> 00:22:35.930
or enter into the brain--

00:22:35.930 --> 00:22:40.270
it might be useful
to review briefly

00:22:40.270 --> 00:22:43.160
the appearance of these
same cranial nerves

00:22:43.160 --> 00:22:45.770
and their nuclei--

00:22:45.770 --> 00:22:50.470
their motor and sensory nuclei--

00:22:50.470 --> 00:22:54.310
in microscopic slides.

00:22:54.310 --> 00:22:55.630
We'll do this very briefly.

00:22:55.630 --> 00:23:01.010
It can of course not substitute
for an accurate examination

00:23:01.010 --> 00:23:05.990
of the slides in total.

00:23:05.990 --> 00:23:09.800
This first level
that you see here

00:23:09.800 --> 00:23:14.040
is typical for the
medulla oblongata--

00:23:14.040 --> 00:23:18.110
the caudal half or so
of the rhombencephalon.

00:23:18.110 --> 00:23:23.960
We see here at the very base of
the brain, the pyramidal tract,

00:23:23.960 --> 00:23:25.610
close to the midline.

00:23:25.610 --> 00:23:30.800
Lateral to it, the
remarkable configuration

00:23:30.800 --> 00:23:35.390
of the inferior
olivary nucleus that

00:23:35.390 --> 00:23:38.570
is responsible for
the bulge that we

00:23:38.570 --> 00:23:44.270
saw in inspecting the
ventral surface of the brain.

00:23:44.270 --> 00:23:49.160
And we see, as we proceed
along the lateral surface,

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:52.760
we see that we come
to a point that there

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:55.350
appears to be a deep valley.

00:23:55.350 --> 00:24:01.070
That deep valley is actually the
floor of the fourth ventricle.

00:24:01.070 --> 00:24:05.750
The choroid membrane that
forms the roof of the fourth

00:24:05.750 --> 00:24:10.370
ventricle cannot be seen here,
because the staining technique

00:24:10.370 --> 00:24:13.010
is unable to bring it out.

00:24:13.010 --> 00:24:17.300
Our main point at
this level is to show

00:24:17.300 --> 00:24:23.780
the relationship of the
hypoglossal nerve that

00:24:23.780 --> 00:24:27.980
emerges below the olive.

00:24:27.980 --> 00:24:33.200
You see it here, emerging
through the grove

00:24:33.200 --> 00:24:36.920
between the pyramidal
tract on the medial side

00:24:36.920 --> 00:24:41.870
and the olivary prominence
on the lateral side.

00:24:41.870 --> 00:24:44.370
We see it's not a nucleus--

00:24:44.370 --> 00:24:48.760
the nucleus from which the
hypoglossal nerve originates,

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:51.200
close to the
midline, immediately

00:24:51.200 --> 00:24:54.061
below the floor of
the fourth ventricle.

00:24:57.360 --> 00:25:01.680
If we compare that
medial exit, that

00:25:01.680 --> 00:25:06.030
is really a very typical
position for a pure motor nerve

00:25:06.030 --> 00:25:08.220
to take its exit--

00:25:08.220 --> 00:25:11.970
close to the ventral
midline, in the same manner

00:25:11.970 --> 00:25:15.870
that characterizes
the motor or ventral

00:25:15.870 --> 00:25:18.270
root in the spinal cord--

00:25:18.270 --> 00:25:24.990
if you compare it with the
exit point of a mixed nerve,

00:25:24.990 --> 00:25:29.380
in this particular
case, the vagus nerve.

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:33.480
The vagus nerve, as
you can see here,

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:36.270
takes a far more lateral exit.

00:25:36.270 --> 00:25:40.580
It is almost in the position
within the spinal cord

00:25:40.580 --> 00:25:44.200
we would call dorsal root.

00:25:44.200 --> 00:25:49.440
And it's cell groups
of origin are several.

00:25:49.440 --> 00:25:52.650
Instead of one
single motor nucleus,

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:57.360
the vagus nerve has no
less than two motor nuclei.

00:25:57.360 --> 00:26:00.840
The main one is lateral
to the hypoglossal nucleus

00:26:00.840 --> 00:26:03.240
in the floor of the
fourth ventricle.

00:26:03.240 --> 00:26:08.350
It is known as the dorsal
motor nucleus of the vagus.

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:12.540
A second motor nucleus
is situated more deeply

00:26:12.540 --> 00:26:17.820
hidden in the substance of
the brainstem tegmentum dorsal

00:26:17.820 --> 00:26:21.980
to the olive, approximately
at this position.

00:26:21.980 --> 00:26:27.240
And from these two sources,
axons unite with each other

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:31.260
and together take
their exit through

00:26:31.260 --> 00:26:35.010
the lateral dorsal aspect
of the medulla oblongata,

00:26:35.010 --> 00:26:42.360
in that characteristic position
in which mixed cranial nerves

00:26:42.360 --> 00:26:45.930
exit from the brain.

00:26:45.930 --> 00:26:51.440
The vagus nerve-- and much
like the glossopharyngeus,

00:26:51.440 --> 00:26:54.780
its immediate
anterior neighbor--

00:26:54.780 --> 00:26:57.990
in addition, contains
sensory fibers.

00:26:57.990 --> 00:27:03.300
These come in at the
same point, and then they

00:27:03.300 --> 00:27:10.380
proceed inside the brainstem to
the so-called solitary tract,

00:27:10.380 --> 00:27:12.870
which is shown here.

00:27:12.870 --> 00:27:15.420
And from here on,
the distribution

00:27:15.420 --> 00:27:19.710
of impulses to the nucleus
of the solitary tract--

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:27.827
and hence to central stations
higher up in the brainstem--

00:27:27.827 --> 00:27:28.410
they'll begin.

00:27:31.700 --> 00:27:34.000
We see here, in other words--

00:27:34.000 --> 00:27:36.510
very clearly illustrated
at this level--

00:27:36.510 --> 00:27:41.340
the differential exit zone of
a pure somatic motor nerve--

00:27:41.340 --> 00:27:43.290
the hypoglossus--

00:27:43.290 --> 00:27:50.120
with a mixed nerve that is
partly motor, partly sensory--

00:27:50.120 --> 00:27:50.910
the nervus vagus.

00:27:56.070 --> 00:28:01.960
On the next slide, we see
the characteristic entrance

00:28:01.960 --> 00:28:06.530
into the brain of a
pure sensory nerve.

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:09.240
The sensory nerve
that is shown here

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.900
is the VIII nerve, in
particular, that component

00:28:12.900 --> 00:28:16.420
that we call the cochlear
nerve, because it collects

00:28:16.420 --> 00:28:19.540
its impulses from the
spinal ganglion, which

00:28:19.540 --> 00:28:22.840
in turn picks up its
impulses from the hair

00:28:22.840 --> 00:28:25.550
cells in Corti's organ.

00:28:25.550 --> 00:28:30.130
We see the nerve here
coming in far lateral

00:28:30.130 --> 00:28:34.690
into a lateral and dorsal
zone of the cross section.

00:28:34.690 --> 00:28:39.855
We see its two recipient
secondary sensory nuclei:

00:28:39.855 --> 00:28:43.990
the ventral cochlear nucleus
and the dorsal cochlear nucleus.

00:28:43.990 --> 00:28:46.630
And from here on
then, the path will

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:52.060
continue as a lemniscal pathway
that will ascend, ultimately,

00:28:52.060 --> 00:28:53.800
to the inferior colliculus.

00:29:03.660 --> 00:29:09.160
This level marks exactly
the pontomedullary groove

00:29:09.160 --> 00:29:14.010
that we saw a moment ago in
inspecting the ventral surface

00:29:14.010 --> 00:29:16.620
of the gross brain.

00:29:16.620 --> 00:29:19.860
If you see here, the
characteristic sequence

00:29:19.860 --> 00:29:24.650
of nerves taking their exit
from the ventral surface

00:29:24.650 --> 00:29:28.880
closest to the midline
is the abducens nerve--

00:29:28.880 --> 00:29:31.260
a pure motor nerve.

00:29:31.260 --> 00:29:33.030
And hence, it
should not surprise

00:29:33.030 --> 00:29:37.410
us to see it take its exit so
close to the ventral midline.

00:29:37.410 --> 00:29:42.240
It too could be compared
to a ventral root

00:29:42.240 --> 00:29:44.730
in the spinal cord.

00:29:44.730 --> 00:29:47.820
As we proceed
laterally from here,

00:29:47.820 --> 00:29:52.500
the next nerve to be
encountered is number VII.

00:29:52.500 --> 00:29:55.260
It's clearly shown
in this preparation,

00:29:55.260 --> 00:29:59.650
taking a more lateral
exit from the brain.

00:29:59.650 --> 00:30:03.090
The seventh nerve
is partly motor--

00:30:03.090 --> 00:30:05.250
is largely motor even--

00:30:05.250 --> 00:30:07.680
but it contains sensory fibers.

00:30:07.680 --> 00:30:10.320
As a mixed nerve it--

00:30:10.320 --> 00:30:14.030
just like the vagus nerve
and the glossopharyngeus--

00:30:14.030 --> 00:30:20.310
takes its exit or entrance
in a more lateral part

00:30:20.310 --> 00:30:24.632
of the brainstem circumference.

00:30:24.632 --> 00:30:28.530
The most lateral
nerve to be seen here

00:30:28.530 --> 00:30:31.365
is the vestibular nerve.

00:30:31.365 --> 00:30:36.060
It is part of the
eighth cranial nerve.

00:30:36.060 --> 00:30:40.490
On the interval between the
facial nerve and the purely

00:30:40.490 --> 00:30:46.410
sensory vestibular nerve, you
see an extra little nerve.

00:30:46.410 --> 00:30:54.720
This extra little nerve is
known as the nervus intermedius

00:30:54.720 --> 00:30:57.270
of Wrisberg.

00:30:57.270 --> 00:31:02.130
This nerve actually
belongs to the complex

00:31:02.130 --> 00:31:03.750
of the facial nerve.

00:31:03.750 --> 00:31:06.570
It is composed of
its autonomic motor

00:31:06.570 --> 00:31:10.970
fibers and its sensory fibers.

00:31:10.970 --> 00:31:13.890
Together, these fibers will--

00:31:13.890 --> 00:31:20.220
once they have left the
confines of the cranium--

00:31:20.220 --> 00:31:24.911
they will appear as the chorda
tympani in the periphery.

00:31:27.800 --> 00:31:32.420
Recapitulating, at
this level we find--

00:31:32.420 --> 00:31:36.950
as the medial
element to emerge--

00:31:36.950 --> 00:31:39.910
a pure somatic motor nerve--

00:31:39.910 --> 00:31:44.660
the nervus abducens--
a mixed nerve--

00:31:44.660 --> 00:31:49.520
the nervus facialis-- with
its sensory component here

00:31:49.520 --> 00:31:54.350
as separately shown, and then in
the farthest lateral position,

00:31:54.350 --> 00:31:55.680
a purely sensory nerve--

00:31:55.680 --> 00:31:57.098
the vestibular.

00:32:01.880 --> 00:32:04.130
This level is included
in this survey

00:32:04.130 --> 00:32:10.160
because it shows the peculiar
mode of exit of the trochlear

00:32:10.160 --> 00:32:11.240
nerve.

00:32:11.240 --> 00:32:15.530
We are here at the level,
just at the caudal border

00:32:15.530 --> 00:32:17.300
of the midbrain.

00:32:17.300 --> 00:32:20.420
This region of the
brainstem is sometimes

00:32:20.420 --> 00:32:22.440
known as the isthmus--

00:32:22.440 --> 00:32:26.000
the narrowest part
of the brainstem.

00:32:26.000 --> 00:32:30.320
One sees here only one
cranial nerve emerging,

00:32:30.320 --> 00:32:35.690
and quite anomalously, it
emerges from the dorsal midline

00:32:35.690 --> 00:32:39.590
of the first crossing
through a thin plate

00:32:39.590 --> 00:32:44.090
that forms the
roof of the isthmus

00:32:44.090 --> 00:32:46.710
of the cerebral aqueduct.

00:32:49.420 --> 00:32:56.720
This thin roof is known as
the anterior medullary velum.

00:32:56.720 --> 00:33:02.400
We see the fiber material of the
left and right trochlear nerves

00:33:02.400 --> 00:33:06.390
crossing through it
before the nerve emerges

00:33:06.390 --> 00:33:10.590
as a peripheral nerve
from the dorsal surface

00:33:10.590 --> 00:33:12.540
of this level of the brainstem.

00:33:18.680 --> 00:33:21.550
This level of the
midbrain corresponds

00:33:21.550 --> 00:33:23.980
to the inferior colliculus.

00:33:23.980 --> 00:33:29.040
One sees the inferior colliculus
as a prominent structure

00:33:29.040 --> 00:33:33.560
forming the roof
of the midbrain.

00:33:33.560 --> 00:33:38.960
Below it, and somewhat
between the left and right

00:33:38.960 --> 00:33:44.120
inferior colliculus, appears
the cerebral aqueduct.

00:33:44.120 --> 00:33:47.930
Surrounding it is a
ring of gray matter,

00:33:47.930 --> 00:33:50.900
the central gray substance.

00:33:50.900 --> 00:33:54.410
Immediately below it,
close to the midline,

00:33:54.410 --> 00:33:59.810
appears the medial
longitudinal fasciculus.

00:33:59.810 --> 00:34:04.010
And nestled in its
dorsal surface,

00:34:04.010 --> 00:34:07.974
is the motor nucleus
of the trochlear nerve.

00:34:07.974 --> 00:34:11.130
The motor nucleus
in other words,

00:34:11.130 --> 00:34:16.429
as you can now understand, lies
a little rostral to the point

00:34:16.429 --> 00:34:18.630
of exit of the trochlear nerve.

00:34:24.330 --> 00:34:27.600
At this highest level
of the brainstem,

00:34:27.600 --> 00:34:31.020
we are in the rostral
part of the midbrain,

00:34:31.020 --> 00:34:34.739
and we see much the
same configuration

00:34:34.739 --> 00:34:37.230
that we saw in the
preceding slide,

00:34:37.230 --> 00:34:42.510
except that the prominent
structure on the dorsal surface

00:34:42.510 --> 00:34:44.699
is no longer the
inferior colliculus.

00:34:44.699 --> 00:34:48.420
We are now at the level of
the superior colliculus.

00:34:48.420 --> 00:34:51.570
Surrounding the
aqueduct, here again, we

00:34:51.570 --> 00:34:55.000
see the central gray substance.

00:34:55.000 --> 00:34:58.530
Close to the midline, along
the ventral circumference

00:34:58.530 --> 00:35:02.400
of the central gray substance--
the medial longitudinal

00:35:02.400 --> 00:35:03.710
fasciculus.

00:35:03.710 --> 00:35:06.540
And the somewhat
light staining zone,

00:35:06.540 --> 00:35:09.240
immediately to the
medial side of it,

00:35:09.240 --> 00:35:14.080
is the motor nucleus of
the oculomotor nerve.

00:35:14.080 --> 00:35:21.090
We see emerging from that motor
nucleus, numerous rootlets.

00:35:21.090 --> 00:35:25.050
We can see them
almost fanning out

00:35:25.050 --> 00:35:27.660
through the midline portion--

00:35:27.660 --> 00:35:31.080
the medial portion-- of
the midbrain tegmentum.

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:34.230
And throughout the
ventral surface,

00:35:34.230 --> 00:35:39.630
we can see how many such
little rootlets combine

00:35:39.630 --> 00:35:44.970
to form the exit rootlets
of the oculomotor nerve

00:35:44.970 --> 00:35:50.760
that emerges between
the cerebral peduncles

00:35:50.760 --> 00:35:52.350
through the
interpeduncular fossa.

00:35:55.625 --> 00:35:59.118
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:36:05.605 --> 00:36:09.098
[BEEPING]

00:36:26.563 --> 00:36:30.056
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:37:51.900 --> 00:37:57.440
Hello, I'm Dr. Walle Nauta,
and I will be-- you might say--

00:37:57.440 --> 00:38:01.910
your tour guide through this
series on the gross anatomy

00:38:01.910 --> 00:38:04.380
of the human brain.

00:38:04.380 --> 00:38:10.870
The series consists of 15 parts
of rather different lengths.

00:38:10.870 --> 00:38:16.190
In a very quick survey, these
various parts or chapters

00:38:16.190 --> 00:38:20.010
can be listed as follows.

00:38:20.010 --> 00:38:23.170
You will see from this
listing that the first six

00:38:23.170 --> 00:38:28.390
parts or chapters of
the series will not

00:38:28.390 --> 00:38:33.190
include any actual dissection
in the literal sense of cutting

00:38:33.190 --> 00:38:35.500
asunder.

00:38:35.500 --> 00:38:39.640
Instead, the first
two parts deal

00:38:39.640 --> 00:38:44.600
with the bony and membranous
envelopes of the brain,

00:38:44.600 --> 00:38:50.410
parts III and IV on tape 2,
with the brain's blood vessels,

00:38:50.410 --> 00:38:55.330
and parts V and VI on tape
3, with the cranial nerves

00:38:55.330 --> 00:38:57.270
entering and leaving the brain.

00:38:59.810 --> 00:39:03.835
It is only in part
VII on tape 4 that we

00:39:03.835 --> 00:39:08.560
will start removing such
surface structures on our way

00:39:08.560 --> 00:39:13.540
into the brain itself,
beginning at the cerebral cortex

00:39:13.540 --> 00:39:15.870
and proceeding from there--

00:39:15.870 --> 00:39:18.280
in tape 5, part VIII--

00:39:18.280 --> 00:39:23.226
into the deeper reaches of
the cerebral hemisphere.

00:39:23.226 --> 00:39:28.180
The separate tape 6, part IX,
is focused on the temporal lobe.

00:39:31.180 --> 00:39:37.310
In parts X on tape 7, and
parts XI and XII on tape 8,

00:39:37.310 --> 00:39:39.690
we will deal with
the white matter

00:39:39.690 --> 00:39:43.810
of the cerebral hemisphere,
the complexity of which

00:39:43.810 --> 00:39:47.050
will make it
necessary to include

00:39:47.050 --> 00:39:52.510
in part XII, the survey of some
histological sections stained

00:39:52.510 --> 00:39:53.130
for myelin.

00:39:56.260 --> 00:39:59.350
Continuing on tape
8, part XIII will

00:39:59.350 --> 00:40:04.050
revisit the arterial circle of
Willis at the base of the brain

00:40:04.050 --> 00:40:06.670
and expose the
various subdivisions

00:40:06.670 --> 00:40:09.460
of the olfactory system.

00:40:09.460 --> 00:40:13.720
In part XIV on tape 9,
we will begin our descent

00:40:13.720 --> 00:40:17.110
from the forebrain to
the midbrain, a descent

00:40:17.110 --> 00:40:22.150
to be continued in the 15th,
and last part, on tape 10

00:40:22.150 --> 00:40:27.384
with the dissection of the
hindbrain and cerebellum.

00:40:27.384 --> 00:40:30.436
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:40:36.380 --> 00:40:41.690
The dura has, in a word, its
own arterial blood supply.

00:40:44.360 --> 00:40:47.660
These blood vessels
are understandably

00:40:47.660 --> 00:40:49.925
called the meningeal arteries.

00:40:52.860 --> 00:40:56.680
The most prominent among them
is the middle meningeal artery,

00:40:56.680 --> 00:41:04.010
which we see here with
several branches distributed

00:41:04.010 --> 00:41:11.550
over the various parts
of the dural cap.

00:41:11.550 --> 00:41:22.150
We can see that this
midline duplicature almost

00:41:22.150 --> 00:41:26.550
subdivides the cranial cavity
into a left and a right half.

00:41:26.550 --> 00:41:32.340
Obviously, it has to have a
relatively wide opening here

00:41:32.340 --> 00:41:36.690
to permit the passage of
connections between the left

00:41:36.690 --> 00:41:39.990
and the right hemisphere.

00:41:39.990 --> 00:41:43.800
We see that here, this
very deep incisure

00:41:43.800 --> 00:41:49.050
between the spinal cord and
brainstem on the one hand,

00:41:49.050 --> 00:41:53.740
and the lower surface of the
cerebellum on the other hand,

00:41:53.740 --> 00:42:01.602
the arachnoid stretches over,
and thereby comes to form

00:42:01.602 --> 00:42:07.290
the cover for a very deep
and large cistern that is

00:42:07.290 --> 00:42:09.370
of great practical importance.

00:42:09.370 --> 00:42:14.490
This is the so-called
cerebellomedullary cistern,

00:42:14.490 --> 00:42:18.444
also called the cisterna magna.

00:42:18.444 --> 00:42:21.930
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:42:25.914 --> 00:42:33.030
Now this vein is often known as
the posterior anastomosing vein

00:42:33.030 --> 00:42:35.280
of Labbé.

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:41.190
And one sees that the way
in which the vein's laid out

00:42:41.190 --> 00:42:44.010
on the surface of
the cortex virtually

00:42:44.010 --> 00:42:48.780
ensures that there are
multiple possibilities

00:42:48.780 --> 00:42:52.260
for directional change
of the blood flow.

00:42:52.260 --> 00:42:55.350
Then a vein would become
obstructed somewhere

00:42:55.350 --> 00:42:57.450
in this area, let us say--

00:42:57.450 --> 00:43:00.810
the blood flow would have no
difficulty finding an alternate

00:43:00.810 --> 00:43:04.830
route into the middle
superficial vein here

00:43:04.830 --> 00:43:09.420
and through Labbé's vein
into the transverse sinus.

00:43:09.420 --> 00:43:14.280
We can see here the curve taken
by the anterior cerebral artery

00:43:14.280 --> 00:43:16.800
coming from this point here--

00:43:16.800 --> 00:43:20.980
the curve around the genu
of the corpus callosum--

00:43:20.980 --> 00:43:26.410
and from there on
moving caudally

00:43:26.410 --> 00:43:30.360
over the surface of
the corpus callosum.

00:43:30.360 --> 00:43:32.140
We'll open this now.

00:43:32.140 --> 00:43:38.100
We see here in the depth,
the corpus callosum here.

00:43:38.100 --> 00:43:42.590
And with a little
bit of inconvenience,

00:43:42.590 --> 00:43:47.640
we can see here one of the
two anterior cerebral arteries

00:43:47.640 --> 00:43:52.280
lying on the dorsal
surface of the callosum.

00:43:52.280 --> 00:43:57.000
And here laterally, the
middle cerebral artery lying

00:43:57.000 --> 00:43:59.940
on the surface of the insular--

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:04.350
quite far away from the brain
surface and communicating

00:44:04.350 --> 00:44:08.460
with it only through
secondary vessels coming off

00:44:08.460 --> 00:44:10.800
of the lips of
the opercula here.

00:44:10.800 --> 00:44:15.110
And finally, the posterior
cerebral artery here,

00:44:15.110 --> 00:44:18.180
that is quite
well-hidden in the groove

00:44:18.180 --> 00:44:21.690
between the medial
surface of the occipital

00:44:21.690 --> 00:44:26.670
and temporal lobes here on
the one hand and the midbrain

00:44:26.670 --> 00:44:28.592
on the other hand.

00:44:28.592 --> 00:44:31.785
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:44:38.020 --> 00:44:43.390
Starting at the caudal
end of the brain,

00:44:43.390 --> 00:44:49.350
at the spinomedullary
junction, we

00:44:49.350 --> 00:44:54.940
note that many cranial
nerves make their exit

00:44:54.940 --> 00:45:02.010
or enter in the most posterior
parts of the brainstem

00:45:02.010 --> 00:45:04.106
and the rhombencephalon.

00:45:07.890 --> 00:45:09.870
The largest cranial nerve--

00:45:09.870 --> 00:45:12.480
number V, the trigeminus--

00:45:12.480 --> 00:45:16.680
is seen here in a slightly
more forward position,

00:45:16.680 --> 00:45:19.050
entering the brain
through the brachium

00:45:19.050 --> 00:45:28.450
pontis or the middle
cerebellar peduncle here.

00:45:28.450 --> 00:45:31.820
We are in the rostral
part of the midbrain,

00:45:31.820 --> 00:45:35.580
and we see much the
same configuration

00:45:35.580 --> 00:45:38.040
that we saw in the
preceding slide,

00:45:38.040 --> 00:45:43.320
except that the prominent
structure on the dorsal surface

00:45:43.320 --> 00:45:46.080
is no longer the inferior
colliculus-- we are now

00:45:46.080 --> 00:45:49.260
at the level of the
superior colliculus.

00:45:49.260 --> 00:45:52.410
Surrounding the
aqueduct, here again, we

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:55.950
see the central gray substance.

00:45:55.950 --> 00:45:59.340
Close to the midline, along
the ventral circumference

00:45:59.340 --> 00:46:01.670
of the central gray substance--

00:46:01.670 --> 00:46:04.560
the medial longitudinal
fasciculus.

00:46:04.560 --> 00:46:07.350
And the somewhat
light staining zone,

00:46:07.350 --> 00:46:10.000
immediately to the
medial side of it,

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:14.412
is the motor nucleus of
the oculomotor nerve.

00:46:14.412 --> 00:46:17.870
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:46:25.290 --> 00:46:30.860
Here, this gyrus is called
the supramarginal gyrus.

00:46:30.860 --> 00:46:37.470
And another one, riding over the
top of this long sulcus here,

00:46:37.470 --> 00:46:40.800
that runs the length
of the temporal lobe

00:46:40.800 --> 00:46:44.540
and is known as the
superior temporal sulcus.

00:46:44.540 --> 00:46:47.810
We see a similar
convolution riding

00:46:47.810 --> 00:46:52.490
over the top of that
superior temporal sulcus,

00:46:52.490 --> 00:46:56.000
and this is often called
the angular gyrus--

00:46:56.000 --> 00:46:57.680
gyrus angularis.

00:47:01.330 --> 00:47:07.170
This fusion of the two
sulci, of the two fissures,

00:47:07.170 --> 00:47:10.630
is deep enough to cause
a marked prominence

00:47:10.630 --> 00:47:13.420
in the medial wall
of the occipital horn

00:47:13.420 --> 00:47:17.800
of the lateral ventricle, as
we will see a bit later on.

00:47:17.800 --> 00:47:22.215
This prominence is known
as the calcar avis--

00:47:22.215 --> 00:47:23.476
the bird's spur.

00:47:26.810 --> 00:47:33.180
We see that the hippocampal
gyrus is here, deeply incised

00:47:33.180 --> 00:47:36.360
by the fusion of the
parieto-occipital

00:47:36.360 --> 00:47:38.370
and the calcarine fissures.

00:47:38.370 --> 00:47:43.680
And This leads to
a marked narrowing

00:47:43.680 --> 00:47:47.310
of the hippocampal gyrus,
known as the isthmus

00:47:47.310 --> 00:47:49.720
of the gyrus fornicatus--

00:47:49.720 --> 00:47:52.660
isthmus gyri-fornicati.

00:47:52.660 --> 00:47:55.686
We see it here in the depths.

00:47:55.686 --> 00:47:59.130
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:48:12.450 --> 00:48:21.240
I will take two of these slices
of cortical tissue in sequence,

00:48:21.240 --> 00:48:24.420
and then we'll stop
for a moment to look

00:48:24.420 --> 00:48:29.160
at the contrast between the
gray matter of the cortex

00:48:29.160 --> 00:48:32.590
and the white matter.

00:48:32.590 --> 00:48:38.970
And at this point, you will see
how the cingulate fasciculus

00:48:38.970 --> 00:48:43.560
reaches the anterior border
of the corpus callosum, which

00:48:43.560 --> 00:48:45.900
is exactly here.

00:48:45.900 --> 00:48:50.150
This is the so-called
genu, and you can see here

00:48:50.150 --> 00:48:54.360
how the fascicular cinguli
bends over the genu

00:48:54.360 --> 00:48:57.150
to disappear from view.

00:48:57.150 --> 00:49:02.160
It distributes itself there
in the subcallosal component

00:49:02.160 --> 00:49:05.400
of the medial cortex.

00:49:05.400 --> 00:49:09.690
These chordate
veins-- you see here

00:49:09.690 --> 00:49:14.700
how you can follow them
into the collecting channel.

00:49:14.700 --> 00:49:19.560
The name of this vein is
the thalamostriate vein.

00:49:19.560 --> 00:49:24.510
It used to be called
the terminal vein.

00:49:24.510 --> 00:49:28.620
At this point, immediately
behind Monro's foramen,

00:49:28.620 --> 00:49:35.160
it makes a sharp curve
medialward here and disappears.

00:49:35.160 --> 00:49:38.560
This curve here, immediately
behind the interventricular

00:49:38.560 --> 00:49:40.250
foramen--

00:49:40.250 --> 00:49:45.740
this curving vein is
known as the venous angle,

00:49:45.740 --> 00:49:50.460
and it serves the
neuroradiologist

00:49:50.460 --> 00:49:57.060
as an orientation for
the most likely position

00:49:57.060 --> 00:49:59.720
of the interventricular foramen.

00:49:59.720 --> 00:50:03.045
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:50:07.810 --> 00:50:11.680
At this level, we are
approaching the anterior limit

00:50:11.680 --> 00:50:13.200
of the pineal gland--

00:50:13.200 --> 00:50:15.760
cella media left and right.

00:50:15.760 --> 00:50:20.350
Here is the cistern of
the velum interpositum

00:50:20.350 --> 00:50:26.020
and two internal cerebral veins
would be positioned about here,

00:50:26.020 --> 00:50:29.420
in the process of
coming together to form

00:50:29.420 --> 00:50:30.640
the great vein of Galen.

00:50:37.780 --> 00:50:40.760
You remember that the
temporal lobe had been

00:50:40.760 --> 00:50:44.820
detached by a series of cuts.

00:50:50.550 --> 00:50:54.640
One such cut involving
the corpus callosum

00:50:54.640 --> 00:50:57.690
and the fornix bundle--

00:50:57.690 --> 00:51:01.630
this is the fornix bundle here--

00:51:01.630 --> 00:51:06.760
and the other one,
a very deep incision

00:51:06.760 --> 00:51:10.930
into the lateral side
of the temporal lobe--

00:51:10.930 --> 00:51:16.690
deep enough to penetrate
into the temporal horn.

00:51:16.690 --> 00:51:22.120
The gyrus dentatus is
really the ultimate end

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:25.450
of the cortical mantle.

00:51:25.450 --> 00:51:29.290
Between Ammon's horn
and the dentate gyrus,

00:51:29.290 --> 00:51:32.060
a very complicated
topography exists.

00:51:32.060 --> 00:51:34.990
We could perhaps best
sum it up by saying

00:51:34.990 --> 00:51:41.470
that the hippocampus essentially
consists of two cortical gyri.

00:51:41.470 --> 00:51:44.110
It is not the same
type of cortex

00:51:44.110 --> 00:51:47.980
that we would find on
most of-- the complexity

00:51:47.980 --> 00:51:50.390
of the brain for
example here, this

00:51:50.390 --> 00:51:52.870
cortex we would call neocortex.

00:51:52.870 --> 00:51:56.860
This cortex at the
extreme medial edge--

00:51:56.860 --> 00:52:00.320
at the medial margin of
the cortical mantle--

00:52:00.320 --> 00:52:05.080
is of a different
composition, and it is often

00:52:05.080 --> 00:52:08.620
referred to as archicortex--

00:52:08.620 --> 00:52:11.022
the arch cortex.

00:52:11.022 --> 00:52:14.410
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:52:19.740 --> 00:52:24.270
At this point, one hopes
that the split-brain

00:52:24.270 --> 00:52:26.280
will be a favorable one.

00:52:31.410 --> 00:52:37.720
It is a rather forcible
pulling one must do here.

00:52:40.950 --> 00:52:48.570
And as you see, here the
insular appears, now freed--

00:52:48.570 --> 00:52:51.750
at least over its
dorsal part here--

00:52:51.750 --> 00:52:56.070
now freed from the
overhanging wall

00:52:56.070 --> 00:53:00.420
of the parietal and
frontal operculum here.

00:53:00.420 --> 00:53:03.060
One sees that the
insular is composed

00:53:03.060 --> 00:53:10.770
of three or four gyri that
are arranged in a fan shape.

00:53:13.290 --> 00:53:16.060
But it is not too
difficult to imagine

00:53:16.060 --> 00:53:19.790
that this more posterior
part of the optic radiation

00:53:19.790 --> 00:53:25.210
here would find its ultimate
distribution in the cortex

00:53:25.210 --> 00:53:32.830
surrounding the calcarine
fissure, which can easily

00:53:32.830 --> 00:53:35.290
be recognized here.

00:53:35.290 --> 00:53:40.360
Part of this radiation, in
other words, goes to the cuneus

00:53:40.360 --> 00:53:40.960
here--

00:53:40.960 --> 00:53:46.322
this area dorsal to
the calcarine fissure.

00:53:46.322 --> 00:53:49.300
And the other part,
the more ventral part,

00:53:49.300 --> 00:53:54.840
goes to the lingual gyrus,
which we see appearing here.

00:53:54.840 --> 00:53:55.970
This is the lingual gyrus.

00:54:01.080 --> 00:54:04.930
Here is the fan here.

00:54:04.930 --> 00:54:07.780
In this part, it's called
the internal capsule.

00:54:07.780 --> 00:54:13.180
In that part, where it lies now
free off the lentiform nucleus,

00:54:13.180 --> 00:54:18.380
here is the corona radiata with
these different radiations,

00:54:18.380 --> 00:54:25.484
including the optic radiation
here in the most caudal part.

00:54:25.484 --> 00:54:28.860
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:54:34.440 --> 00:54:38.530
I will make a somewhat
random cut here,

00:54:38.530 --> 00:54:42.750
trying to stay as
much as I can parallel

00:54:42.750 --> 00:54:46.016
to the fibers of
the optic radiation.

00:54:50.950 --> 00:54:57.310
When we do that and
now open up here,

00:54:57.310 --> 00:55:00.040
it is quite clear
that we are looking

00:55:00.040 --> 00:55:01.990
into a lateral ventricle.

00:55:01.990 --> 00:55:05.700
We see choroid
plexus appear here.

00:55:08.470 --> 00:55:12.730
I can easily guess that
what I am looking at here

00:55:12.730 --> 00:55:15.850
is the calcar avis.

00:55:15.850 --> 00:55:21.730
We see here then, once
more, the carotid artery--

00:55:21.730 --> 00:55:24.590
this of the left side,
that one of the right side.

00:55:24.590 --> 00:55:28.315
Let us quickly get oriented
in the basal aspect

00:55:28.315 --> 00:55:30.340
of the diencephalon.

00:55:30.340 --> 00:55:33.130
Here are the optic
nerves undergoing

00:55:33.130 --> 00:55:35.050
their hemidecussation.

00:55:35.050 --> 00:55:40.540
This is the tuber cinereum
with the pituitary stock

00:55:40.540 --> 00:55:44.230
invariably here disconnected
from the pituitary that

00:55:44.230 --> 00:55:50.650
remained inside the skull
underneath the diaphragm

00:55:50.650 --> 00:55:52.906
of the sella turcica.

00:55:52.906 --> 00:55:56.350
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:56:01.780 --> 00:56:05.360
Here is the so-called
massa intermedia,

00:56:05.360 --> 00:56:08.260
a bridge of gray
matter interconnecting

00:56:08.260 --> 00:56:10.420
the thalamus of the left
side with the thalamus

00:56:10.420 --> 00:56:12.130
of the right side.

00:56:12.130 --> 00:56:16.240
All this is the third ventricle.

00:56:16.240 --> 00:56:22.120
Here is its communication
with the lateral ventricle

00:56:22.120 --> 00:56:28.570
and here its communication
with the fourth ventricle here.

00:56:28.570 --> 00:56:30.130
This is the Sylvian aqueduct.

00:56:33.400 --> 00:56:38.320
When we look into the markedly
dilated third ventricle here,

00:56:38.320 --> 00:56:42.560
it is clear that in this case,
there is a well-developed massa

00:56:42.560 --> 00:56:43.970
intermedia.

00:56:43.970 --> 00:56:48.500
The massa intermedia
is not always present.

00:56:48.500 --> 00:56:55.940
It appears to be missing in
approximately 10% of humans.

00:56:55.940 --> 00:57:00.830
But you see it here, it is
nothing very voluminous,

00:57:00.830 --> 00:57:06.296
but it is an interesting
detail of thalamic anatomy.

00:57:08.972 --> 00:57:15.750
I now turn the brain on
its side, and at this point

00:57:15.750 --> 00:57:19.320
we are beginning to
see the lateral surface

00:57:19.320 --> 00:57:22.230
of the midbrain.

00:57:22.230 --> 00:57:27.090
We see here, looking at the
thalamus once more from behind,

00:57:27.090 --> 00:57:33.480
here is the inferior
colliculus here,

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:37.110
the anterior colliculus
here, the thalamus,

00:57:37.110 --> 00:57:39.060
the lateral geniculate body--

00:57:39.060 --> 00:57:44.250
This overhanging
large triangular area

00:57:44.250 --> 00:57:47.280
of the thalamus, that
lies in the vertex

00:57:47.280 --> 00:57:53.160
of the curve of the thalamus,
is the so-called pulvinar.

00:57:53.160 --> 00:57:57.750
The name pulvinar meant
pillow, and apparently

00:57:57.750 --> 00:58:00.810
those who saw the structure
for the first time

00:58:00.810 --> 00:58:02.760
were impressed with it.

00:58:02.760 --> 00:58:08.810
It reminded them of a pillow
slung over the back of a chair.

00:58:08.810 --> 00:58:12.485
And here we see
it, the pulvinar.

00:58:12.485 --> 00:58:15.950
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:58:23.870 --> 00:58:27.230
I will cut the midbrain
here at the level

00:58:27.230 --> 00:58:30.640
between the superior
and inferior colliculi.

00:58:40.740 --> 00:58:49.550
Obviously, this cut would have
to involve the basilar artery.

00:58:53.690 --> 00:58:55.400
Needless to say,
what we will find

00:58:55.400 --> 00:58:58.720
here just lateral
to the thalamus

00:58:58.720 --> 00:59:02.690
is the tail of the
caudate nucleus here.

00:59:02.690 --> 00:59:07.670
And quite in general, we see
and are reminded rather clearly

00:59:07.670 --> 00:59:11.640
of the fact that this
whole region here

00:59:11.640 --> 00:59:17.290
marks the roof of the temporal
horn of the ventricle.

00:59:21.990 --> 00:59:25.950
We will now turn our
attention to the posterior

00:59:25.950 --> 00:59:29.045
part of the brainstem--

00:59:29.045 --> 00:59:33.640
the rhombencephalon
with the cerebellum.

00:59:33.640 --> 00:59:37.320
You can clearly see here
the typical configuration

00:59:37.320 --> 00:59:39.220
of the dentate nucleus.

00:59:39.220 --> 00:59:47.660
It is composed of a strongly
folded membrane of--

00:59:47.660 --> 00:59:52.040
it's a sheet of
gray matter here.

00:59:52.040 --> 00:59:57.270
And out from its hilus
emerge the myelinated fibers

00:59:57.270 --> 00:59:59.640
of the brachium conjunctivum.

00:59:59.640 --> 01:00:04.020
Here, the brachium conjunctivum
has left the cerebellum

01:00:04.020 --> 01:00:05.700
over this stretch.

01:00:05.700 --> 01:00:12.570
Here, it disappears under the
roof of the midbrain tectum

01:00:12.570 --> 01:00:16.600
here, and at this
point we see once more

01:00:16.600 --> 01:00:20.010
the complete decussation
of the cistern.

01:00:20.010 --> 01:00:25.950
The brachium conjunctivum, or
anterior cerebellar peduncle,

01:00:25.950 --> 01:00:29.330
is a completely closed cistern.

